# AWS CCP — Cloud Concepts

Which of the six advantages of cloud computing means you pay only for what you consume instead of buying data centers up front?
---
Trade fixed expense for variable expense
~ Go global in minutes
~ Stop guessing capacity
~ Benefit from massive economies of scale
---
The classic capex-to-opex argument. No hardware purchase before you know your demand.

Which cloud advantage means you no longer estimate infrastructure needs before launch?
---
Stop guessing capacity
~ Increase speed and agility
~ Trade fixed expense for variable expense
~ Benefit from massive economies of scale
---
Scale with actual demand instead of over-provisioning or hitting a ceiling.

Because AWS aggregates usage from many customers, prices fall as it grows. This advantage is called massive {{economies of scale}}.
---
Hundreds of thousands of customers sharing infrastructure drives per-unit cost down.

Which cloud deployment model connects on-premises infrastructure to cloud resources?
---
Hybrid
~ Public cloud
~ Private cloud
~ Multi-cloud
---
Typically linked with a Site-to-Site VPN or Direct Connect.

In which service model do you manage the operating system and everything above it, while the provider manages the hardware?
---
IaaS
~ PaaS
~ SaaS
~ On-premises
---
Infrastructure as a Service. EC2 is the classic example.

In which service model do you bring only code and data, while the provider runs the platform underneath?
---
PaaS
~ IaaS
~ SaaS
~ On-premises
---
Platform as a Service. Elastic Beanstalk is the AWS example.

Which service model delivers a finished application you simply use, like web email?
---
SaaS
~ IaaS
~ PaaS
~ FaaS
---
Software as a Service. You manage nothing but your data and users.

How many pillars does the AWS Well-Architected Framework have?
---
6
~ 4
~ 5
~ 7
---
Operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, sustainability.

Which Well-Architected pillar is about recovering from failure and scaling to meet demand?
---
Reliability
~ Performance efficiency
~ Operational excellence
~ Cost optimization
---
Multi-AZ deployments and automatic recovery are its bread and butter.

Which Well-Architected pillar, added in 2021, is about minimizing environmental impact?
---
Sustainability
~ Cost optimization
~ Performance efficiency
~ Reliability
---
The sixth and newest pillar.

The ability to automatically acquire resources as demand rises and release them as it falls is called {{elasticity}}.
---
Scalability is being able to grow. Elasticity is doing it automatically, in both directions.

Adding more instances to share the load is called {{horizontal}} scaling.
---
Also called scaling out. Vertical scaling (scaling up) means a bigger instance instead.

A system that keeps operating with zero downtime even while components fail is called {{fault tolerant}}.
---
High availability allows a brief interruption during failover. Fault tolerance allows none.

Which migration strategy moves an application to the cloud unchanged, also known as lift and shift?
---
Rehost
~ Replatform
~ Refactor
~ Repurchase
---
The fastest of the 7 Rs. No code changes.

Which migration strategy makes a few cloud optimizations without changing the core architecture?
---
Replatform
~ Rehost
~ Refactor
~ Retain
---
Lift, tinker, and shift. For example moving the database to RDS on the way.

Which migration strategy redesigns an application to be cloud-native?
---
Refactor
~ Rehost
~ Replatform
~ Repurchase
---
Also called re-architect. Most expensive up front, biggest long-term payoff.

Which migration strategy replaces a self-hosted application with a SaaS product?
---
Repurchase
~ Retire
~ Retain
~ Relocate
---
Drop and shop. The remaining Rs: retire it, retain it on-prem, or relocate (e.g. VMware to AWS).

Which AWS framework guides cloud migration across Business, People, Governance, Platform, Security, and Operations perspectives?
---
Cloud Adoption Framework
= CAF
= AWS CAF
= the Cloud Adoption Framework
---
The CAF is about organizational readiness. Well-Architected is about workload design.

An AWS {{Region}} is a physical location in the world consisting of multiple isolated Availability Zones.
---
Regions are fully independent of each other. Pick one near your users or where the law requires.

What is an Availability Zone?
---
One or more discrete data centers with redundant power and networking
~ A country where AWS operates
~ A CloudFront cache location
~ A subnet inside a VPC
---
AZs in a Region are close enough for low-latency links but isolated from each other's failures.

Every AWS Region contains at least how many Availability Zones?
---
3
~ 1
~ 2
~ 6
---
Spreading a workload across multiple AZs is the standard high-availability pattern.

Which part of the AWS global infrastructure caches content close to users for CloudFront?
---
Edge locations
~ Availability Zones
~ Regions
~ Local Zones
---
There are hundreds of edge locations, far more than Regions.
